      "writtenOn": "2025-09-24T13:35:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If a CPU is about to set `pkvm_initialized` but hasn\u0027t set it yet, it means the host is still trusted, and is not expected to issue these hypercalls, right?",
      "parentUuid": "7d90d9ae_0b0af281",
      "revId": "d3af314c3de63da776d34363949c57fb68b3fabc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "238f3952_a58ab653",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/init_finalise.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 380,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-09-24T13:46:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What I meant is this scenario:\ncpu1: at init_finalize.c:378 (checking the value of success and success is true)\ncpu2: at init_finalize.c:332 (in pkvm_reprivilege_vcpu checking is_pkvm_initialized())\ncpu2 will pass the check and start reprivileging. Right?\n\nWhat I mean is, there is a small time window where pkvm is sure to set `pkvm_initialized` but lost to another cpu which checked before setting it.",
      "parentUuid": "3dafa58a_9bf68bfc",
      "revId": "d3af314c3de63da776d34363949c57fb68b3fabc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1beb59c4_85867a79",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/init_finalise.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 380,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-09-24T13:58:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "As I said, this scenario shouldn\u0027t occur in the first place. The host shouldn\u0027t issue COMMIT_FINALISE(success\u003dtrue) hypercall until everything is successfully initialized and thus there is no need to reprivilege. Right?",
      "parentUuid": "238f3952_a58ab653",
      "revId": "d3af314c3de63da776d34363949c57fb68b3fabc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "845efc5e_a1344025",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/init_finalise.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 380,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-09-24T14:10:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sorry I didn\u0027t understand. The goal of this CL is to cause PKVM_HC_INIT_FINALISE, __PKVM_HC_COMMIT_FINALISE, and __PKVM_HC_REPRIVILEGE_VCPU to fail if `pkvm_initialized` is set to true right? If I understand correctly, the goal is - unintended or malicious invocation of the above hypercalls should not succeed if `pkvm_initialized` is true. We are trying to close a time window when the host sets ``pkvm_initialized` but other cpus fail to observe it. If this is the goal, then IMHO there is no practical difference between the cpu at init_finalize:378 (checking success) or at init_finalize: 380 (setting pkvm_initialized to true).\n\nThe scenario shouldn\u0027t occur at the first place and if we trust the host, this CL might not be needed. return from hypercall is also an implicit memory barrier and any further hypercalls from the host after this hypercall returns to host should be safe I guess.",
      "parentUuid": "1beb59c4_85867a79",
      "revId": "d3af314c3de63da776d34363949c57fb68b3fabc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "65b81e52_92d8bb94",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/init_finalise.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 380,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-09-24T15:06:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e return from hypercall is also an implicit memory barrier and any further hypercalls from the host after this hypercall returns to host should be safe I guess.\n\nHmm yeah, good point. If so, I\u0027m happy to abandon this patch. Or perhaps just use WRITE_ONCE/READ_ONCE.\n\nBut I\u0027m not entirely sure... We need a barrier on the read side as well, and on the read side we need it _after_ reading `pkvm_initialized`?\n\n```\n#define __smp_load_acquire(p)\t\t\t\t\t\t\\\n({\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\\n\ttypeof(*p) ___p1 \u003d READ_ONCE(*p);\t\t\t\t\\\n\tcompiletime_assert_atomic_type(*p);\t\t\t\t\\\n\tbarrier();\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\\n\t___p1;\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\\n})\n```",
      "parentUuid": "845efc5e_a1344025",
      "revId": "d3af314c3de63da776d34363949c57fb68b3fabc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "79e1c95e_f6cea7b5",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/init_finalise.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
